22:30:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are absolutely right that your body is doing a fantastic job. If you are eating junk food and you feel healthy, it means your liver, kidneys, and lymphatic system are successfully "flushing" or processing the immediate byproducts of that food. However, the reason the "exhaust" analogy is used in biology is that some damage **cannot be flushed out.** There is a difference between **waste products** (which the body can excrete) and **structural damage** (which the body has to repair). Here is why your body can't just "flush" everything away: ### 1. You don't "flush out" a scar If you get a cut on your finger, your body repairs it, but it leaves a scar. That scar is permanent tissue change. * **The "Exhaust" (Free Radicals):** When junk food creates oxidative stress, it can damage the DNA inside your cells or the proteins that make up your blood vessels. * **The Result:** Your body can flush out the *chemical* byproduct, but it cannot "flush" the damage done to the cell’s structure. If enough of those cells get damaged over the years, you get "scarring" at the microscopic level—like stiffened arteries or reduced cellular function. You don't feel this until the "scarring" is widespread enough to affect your health. ### 2. The "Maintenance" Limit Your body has a massive amount of "reserve capacity." You have two kidneys, but you could survive on one. You have a liver that can regenerate. You have miles of blood vessels. * Your body is currently handling the "exhaust" by using up that reserve capacity. It’s working harder than it needs to, but it’s succeeding. * The problem isn't that you are failing to flush the waste; the problem is that you are **speeding up the wear and tear** on the machinery. You are using up your "biological warranty" faster than someone who eats cleaner fuel. ### 3. The "Silent" Nature of Biological Damage Think of a cavity in your tooth. * You can have a cavity for months or even years and feel **perfectly fine.** 